# One turn of the Elm loop for a host that is not in this process.
#
# `turn` is pure and is the whole of the boundary's behaviour: a request line
# in, a reply line out, and in between exactly `view`, `route.at`, `update`,
# `view` again and `diff`. `serve` is the only `!io` in this package, the same
# split `tea_term` has, and it does two things `turn` cannot: it reads and
# writes the lines, and it stands between a failing application and the host.
#
# Why the model travels. The mutable model remains owned by the host: it
# arrives with the request and leaves with the reply, as text the application
# encodes. `serve_with_state` may separately retain read-only init data inside
# the guest, but neither `turn` nor `turn_with_flags` has such an input. Their
# useful consequence is that a turn is a function of its inputs and of nothing
# else, so the same transcript replays identically on a browser, on node and
# on the JVM -- which is what makes this testable without a browser at all.
#
# Why `serve` is a loop over lines when the whole design says the guest has no
# loop. It is a loop over *the input it was handed*, not over time: a reactor
# turn writes one line to the guest and the guest drains it, answers, reaches
# end of input and returns. The same binary run from a terminal drains a whole
# session instead, which is why `dawn run` can drive this program and why the
# example-main contract can hold it to a transcript.
#
# What `catch_panic` is doing here. An application's `update` or `view` may
# panic, and on wasm that panic has to land somewhere: an unhandled one aborts
# the module, and a host holding an aborted instance has lost the model. So
# the guest catches at its own boundary and answers with an error reply, the
# host keeps the model it already had, and the page stays alive. This is the
# consumer for the wasm failure runtime -- the A1 shadow stack that makes a
# raise on wasm32-wasi land at a handler instead of aborting -- and the
# harness has a case that panics on purpose to hold it.
#
# The catch is here rather than inside `turn` because the failure barriers are
# pinned to `!io` (spec 9.8): `catch_panic` has an `!io` row whatever it
# guards, so a `turn` that caught its own failures could not be a pure
# function, and a pure `turn` is what the test blocks below assert on with
# `==`.

use std/io
use std/reactor as session
use std/str
use tea_core/diff.{Patch, Replace, diff}
use node.{Node, Text, Elem, On, NoData, Value, Key, deliver}
use route
use wire.{Request, Init, Event, Bad, decode_request, enc_node, reply_ok, reply_err}

## Answer one request line.
##
## `init` is the model a fresh page starts from, `encode`/`decode` are the
## application's own opaque text for a model, and `update`/`view` are the two
## pure functions `tea_core/app.App` names. They arrive as parameters rather
## than through the trait for the reason `tea_core/app` records at length: a
## generic function over `A: App` can do nothing with an `A.View`, and the
## reconciler needs `Eq` on the tree, which an associated type cannot carry.
##
## The two requests:
##
##   `init`   the host has an empty container. The reply is the initial model
##            and one `replace` at the root, which is the only patch that can
##            describe "there was nothing and now there is a document".
##   `event`  the host names an address, an event and at most one string. The
##            listener is looked up in `view(decode(model))` -- the tree the
##            host is holding, since the host's document is that tree by
##            construction -- the string is handed to that listener's `to_msg`,
##            `update` folds the result in, and the reply is the new model plus
##            `diff(old, new)`.
##
## `M: Eq` and nothing else. The `Fill` bound this used to carry is gone with
## the trait: a listener holds the function, so there is nothing for an
## application to declare.
##
## The `Eq` that stayed is no longer about listeners, and it is worth saying
## why it stayed anyway. `diff` needs `Eq[Node[M]]`, and `Node[M]` has no
## `impl Eq` of its own, so the compiler synthesises one from the structure;
## the synthesis asks every type parameter of the ADT for a bound, whether or
## not the parameter reaches a comparable position. Here it does not: `M`
## occurs only under `On[M]`, whose `impl Eq` reads two fields and never
## touches it. `impl[M] Tree[Node[M]]` and `route.at` therefore lost the bound
## outright; this one survives the change for a reason that has nothing to do
## with what a listener carries. An application pays nothing for it, since an
## ordinary message ADT is structurally comparable without declaring anything.
##
## An application that wants what the page knew before it existed takes
## `turn_with_flags` instead. This one ignores the `flags` an init may carry,
## which is the whole difference between the two and is why an application
## that never asked for flags cannot be given any: its `init` is a value, and
## a value has nowhere to put a string.
pub fn turn[Mo, M](
  line: String,
  init: Mo,
  encode: fn(Mo) -> String,
  decode: fn(String) -> Mo,
  update: fn(Mo, M) -> Mo,
  view: fn(Mo) -> Node[M],
) -> String = turn_with_flags(line, _flags => init, encode, decode, update, view)

## Answer one request line, with the initial model computed from the flags the
## host sent.
##
## The one difference from `turn`: `init` is `fn(Option[String]) -> Mo` rather
## than an `Mo`, and it is called once per `init` request with whatever the
## line's `flags` field held. `None` is a line with no such field, which is
## every line every host writes today, so `turn(line, m, ..)` is exactly
## `turn_with_flags(line, _ => m, ..)` and is written as that below.
##
## Why a function of the flags rather than a model plus a patch step
## (`fn(Mo, String) -> Mo`), which was the other shape that fits. A model built
## from flags often has no meaningful value without them -- a palette with no
## index has nothing to search, a session-scoped view has no user -- and the
## patch shape forces one into existence and then hopes every field gets
## overwritten. Elm's `init : flags -> model` is the same call for the same
## reason, and it makes "there were no flags" a case the application writes
## rather than a state it has to detect.
##
## Why `Option[String]` and not `String`. Absent and empty are different
## answers everywhere else on this wire, and they are different here too: a
## host that has nothing to say omits the field, and a host that has an empty
## string to say sends one. An application that does not care collapses them
## in one line; one that does cannot get the distinction back afterwards.
pub fn turn_with_flags[Mo, M](
  line: String,
  init: fn(Option[String]) -> Mo,
  encode: fn(Mo) -> String,
  decode: fn(String) -> Mo,
  update: fn(Mo, M) -> Mo,
  view: fn(Mo) -> Node[M],
) -> String =
  match decode_request(line) {
    Bad(reason) -> reply_err("bad-request", reason)
    Init(flags) -> {
      let m0 = init(flags)
      let root: Patch[Node[M]] = Patch { path: [], op: Replace(w: view(m0)) }
      reply_ok(encode(m0), [root])
    }
    Event(model, path, event, payload) -> {
      let m0 = decode(model)
      let old = view(m0)
      match route.at(old, path, event) {
        None ->
          reply_err(
            "no-handler",
            "nothing at " ++ show_path(path) ++ " listens for `" ++ event ++ "`",
          )
        Some(listener) ->
          match settle(listener, payload) {
            Err(why) -> reply_err("bad-request", why)
            Ok(msg) -> {
              let m1 = update(m0, msg)
              reply_ok(encode(m1), diff(old, view(m1)))
            }
          }
      }
    }
  }

## Answer one request while a read-only value lives in the guest rather than
## in the model text crossing the wire.
##
## `init` splits the optional flags into `(retained, model)`. Only the model is
## passed to `encode` and `decode`; `update` and `view` receive the retained
## value separately, and `update` cannot return a replacement for it. The first
## component of this function's answer is therefore an init-time replacement:
## `Some` only after a successful `Init`, and `None` on every `Event` and every
## refusal. `serve_with_state` is the sole place that commits that replacement.
##
## The current state is explicit so this remains a pure function. Besides
## making each branch testable, that keeps retained state out of the request
## and reply JSON by construction: the only wire codec here is the `Mo` codec.
pub fn turn_with_state[S, Mo, M](
  line: String,
  state: Option[S],
  init: fn(Option[String]) -> (S, Mo),
  encode: fn(Mo) -> String,
  decode: fn(String) -> Mo,
  update: fn(S, Mo, M) -> Mo,
  view: fn(S, Mo) -> Node[M],
) -> (Option[S], String) =
  match decode_request(line) {
    Bad(reason) -> (None, reply_err("bad-request", reason))
    Init(flags) -> {
      let (kept, m0) = init(flags)
      let root: Patch[Node[M]] = Patch { path: [], op: Replace(w: view(kept, m0)) }
      (Some(kept), reply_ok(encode(m0), [root]))
    }
    Event(model, path, event, payload) ->
      match state {
        None ->
          (None, reply_err("bad-request", "an `event` needs a successful `init` first"))
        Some(kept) -> {
          let m0 = decode(model)
          let old = view(kept, m0)
          match route.at(old, path, event) {
            None ->
              (None, reply_err(
                "no-handler",
                "nothing at " ++ show_path(path) ++ " listens for `" ++ event ++ "`",
              ))
            Some(listener) ->
              match settle(listener, payload) {
                Err(why) -> (None, reply_err("bad-request", why))
                Ok(msg) -> {
                  let m1 = update(kept, m0, msg)
                  (None, reply_ok(encode(m1), diff(old, view(kept, m1))))
                }
              }
          }
        }
      }
  }

# What arrived against what was asked for, and the message that results.
#
# A mismatch is refused rather than defaulted or dropped. The listener said
# what it wanted and the host was told; a payload arriving where none was
# declared, or missing where one was, means the two are looking at different
# trees, which is the same desync `route.at` answering `None` reports and gets
# the same treatment. Papering over it would turn a wrong reading -- a host
# that sends `ev.key` where `ev.target.value` was asked for, say -- into a
# message quietly built from the wrong text, and the transcripts would go on
# being green.
#
# A `NoData` listener is handed `""` rather than given a second shape. It
# declared that it wants nothing, so what it is handed cannot matter, and
# `on_click` builds a function that ignores its argument.
fn settle[M](listener: On[M], payload: Option[String]) -> Result[M, String] =
  match (listener.payload, payload) {
    (NoData, None) -> Ok(deliver(listener, ""))
    (NoData, Some(_)) ->
      Err("`" ++ listener.event ++ "` asks for no payload, but one arrived")
    (Value, Some(text)) -> Ok(deliver(listener, text))
    (Key, Some(text)) -> Ok(deliver(listener, text))
    (_, None) -> Err("`" ++ listener.event ++ "` asks for a payload, but none arrived")
  }

fn show_path(p: List[Int]) -> String = "[" ++ join_ints(p, 0, "") ++ "]"

fn join_ints(p: List[Int], i: Int, acc: String) -> String =
  if i >= len(p) {
    acc
  } else {
    join_ints(p, i + 1, if i == 0 { to_string(p[i]) } else { acc ++ "," ++ to_string(p[i]) })
  }

## Answer every request on stdin, one line each way, until end of input.
##
## A failing application is answered rather than propagated: `catch_panic` is
## an isolation point (spec 9.8), so a panic in `update` or `view` becomes an
## error reply and the loop goes on to the next line. A blank line is skipped,
## because a host that writes "\n" between messages should not be told it sent
## a malformed request.
pub fn serve[Mo, M](
  init: Mo,
  encode: fn(Mo) -> String,
  decode: fn(String) -> Mo,
  update: fn(Mo, M) -> Mo,
  view: fn(Mo) -> Node[M],
) -> Unit !io =
  match io.read_line() {
    None -> ()
    Some(line) -> {
      if str.trim(line) != "" {
        match catch_panic(() => turn(line, init, encode, decode, update, view)) {
          Ok(reply) -> io.println(reply)
          # `e.kind` is the runtime's own word for the failure and it is not
          # the same word on every backend: the JVM answers
          # `dawn.rt.PanicError` where the C runtime answers `panic`. The
          # boundary must not vary by backend -- one transcript has to replay
          # on the JVM, on native and on wasm -- and `catch_panic` catches
          # panics and nothing else, so the kind carries no information the
          # wire word does not. The message is passed through verbatim.
          Err(e) -> io.println(reply_err("panic", e.message))
        }
      }
      serve(init, encode, decode, update, view)
    }
  }

## `serve`, for an application whose initial model is a function of the flags
## the host sent. `turn_with_flags` is the difference and the only one.
##
## The loop is written out a second time rather than factored with `serve`
## into a shared pump. That is deliberate and it is about evidence, not about
## taste: `serve` is what four recorded transcripts replay through, and
## leaving its bytes untouched makes those transcripts evidence about code
## that did not change rather than about a refactor that happened to keep
## them green. The two bodies differ in one identifier, and the day a third
## joins them is the day to factor all three at once.
pub fn serve_with_flags[Mo, M](
  init: fn(Option[String]) -> Mo,
  encode: fn(Mo) -> String,
  decode: fn(String) -> Mo,
  update: fn(Mo, M) -> Mo,
  view: fn(Mo) -> Node[M],
) -> Unit !io =
  match io.read_line() {
    None -> ()
    Some(line) -> {
      if str.trim(line) != "" {
        match catch_panic(() => turn_with_flags(line, init, encode, decode, update, view)) {
          Ok(reply) -> io.println(reply)
          Err(e) -> io.println(reply_err("panic", e.message))
        }
      }
      serve_with_flags(init, encode, decode, update, view)
    }
  }

## `serve_with_flags`, with the init-only part retained by the guest across
## calls to a wasm reactor's `dawn_turn` as well as across lines on the JVM.
##
## The runtime slot is reached only through `std/reactor`'s typed pump. A turn
## is caught before its optional replacement is committed, so a panic during a
## new init leaves the earlier state installed. Blank lines preserve the old
## loop contract: they produce no reply and do not replace or mutate the slot
## (the pump may still read its installed Root).
pub fn serve_with_state[S, Mo, M](
  init: fn(Option[String]) -> (S, Mo),
  encode: fn(Mo) -> String,
  decode: fn(String) -> Mo,
  update: fn(S, Mo, M) -> Mo,
  view: fn(S, Mo) -> Node[M],
) -> Unit !io =
  session.serve((state: Option[S], line: String) => {
    if str.trim(line) == "" {
      let no_replacement: Option[S] = None
      let no_reply: Option[String] = None
      (no_replacement, no_reply)
    } else {
      let (replacement, reply) =
        turn_with_state(line, state, init, encode, decode, update, view)
      (replacement, Some(reply))
    }
  }, e => reply_err("panic", e.message))

# ---- tests -------------------------------------------------------------
#
# A counter, because the smallest app that has a model, two messages and a
# tree with a stable region is enough to pin every branch of a turn. It also
# has a field, which is what the payload branches need: `Set` carries what was
# typed, and the input's listener is `Set` itself.

type Counter = { n: Int }

type Msg =
  | Inc
  | Dec
  | Set(text: String)
derive Show

fn enc(m: Counter) -> String = to_string(m.n)

fn dec(s: String) -> Counter =
  match parse_int(s) {
    Some(n) -> Counter { n: n }
    None -> Counter { n: 0 }
  }

fn upd(m: Counter, msg: Msg) -> Counter =
  match msg {
    Inc -> Counter { n: m.n + 1 }
    Dec -> Counter { n: m.n - 1 }
    Set(text) ->
      match parse_int(text) {
        Some(n) -> Counter { n: n }
        None -> m
      }
  }

fn vw(m: Counter) -> Node[Msg] =
  Elem(tag: "div", props: [], on: [], kids: [
    Elem(
      tag: "button",
      props: [],
      on: [On { event: "click", payload: NoData, to_msg: _p => Dec }],
      kids: [Text(s: "-")],
      key: None,
    ),
    Elem(tag: "span", props: [], on: [], kids: [Text(s: to_string(m.n))], key: None),
    Elem(
      tag: "button",
      props: [],
      on: [On { event: "click", payload: NoData, to_msg: _p => Inc }],
      kids: [Text(s: "+")],
      key: None,
    ),
    # Carries no `value` prop on purpose: the assertions below are about one
    # region of the tree changing per turn, and a field that renders the model
    # would put a second patch in every one of them.
    Elem(
      tag: "input",
      props: [],
      on: [On { event: "input", payload: Value, to_msg: Set }],
      kids: [],
      key: None,
    ),
  ], key: None)

fn ask(line: String) -> String =
  turn(line, Counter { n: 0 }, enc, dec, upd, vw)

test "init answers the initial model and one replace at the root" {
  let out = ask("{\"op\":\"init\"}")
  assert str.starts_with(out, "{\"ok\":true,\"model\":\"0\",\"patches\":[{\"path\":[],\"op\":\"replace\"")
  # The patch carries the whole document, which is what a host with an empty
  # container needs and the only time the whole document crosses.
  assert str.contains(out, "\"kids\":[{\"t\":\"text\",\"s\":\"0\"}]")
}

# The same counter, started from what the host knew. A model that reads its
# whole self out of the flags is the smallest thing that can show the string
# reaching `init` and reaching the view, which is what the assertions below
# and the `tea_dom_flags` session both need.
fn from_flags(flags: Option[String]) -> Counter =
  match flags {
    Some(text) -> dec(text)
    None -> Counter { n: 0 }
  }

fn ask_flagged(line: String) -> String =
  turn_with_flags(line, from_flags, enc, dec, upd, vw)

type Kept = { label: String, by: Int }

fn split_state(flags: Option[String]) -> (Kept, Counter) =
  match flags {
    Some("blue") -> (Kept { label: "blue", by: 3 }, Counter { n: 0 })
    _ -> (Kept { label: "plain", by: 1 }, Counter { n: 0 })
  }

fn state_upd(state: Kept, m: Counter, msg: Msg) -> Counter =
  match msg {
    Inc -> Counter { n: m.n + state.by }
    Dec -> Counter { n: m.n - state.by }
    Set(text) -> upd(m, Set(text: text))
  }

fn state_vw(state: Kept, m: Counter) -> Node[Msg] =
  Elem(tag: "button", props: [("data-state", state.label)], on: [
    On { event: "click", payload: NoData, to_msg: _p => Inc },
  ], kids: [Text(s: to_string(m.n))], key: None)

fn ask_state(line: String, state: Option[Kept]) -> (Option[Kept], String) =
  turn_with_state(line, state, split_state, enc, dec, state_upd, state_vw)

test "flags reach init, and the model they build is the one that goes out" {
  let out = ask_flagged("{\"op\":\"init\",\"flags\":\"7\"}")
  assert str.starts_with(out, "{\"ok\":true,\"model\":\"7\",\"patches\":[{\"path\":[],\"op\":\"replace\"")
  # And into the document, not just into the reply's model field: the root
  # patch is `view(init(flags))`, so a flag the view never reads would leave
  # this line alone.
  assert str.contains(out, "\"kids\":[{\"t\":\"text\",\"s\":\"7\"}]")
}

test "retained state is installed only by init and never enters the model text" {
  let (replacement, out) = ask_state("{\"op\":\"init\",\"flags\":\"blue\"}", None)
  assert replacement == Some(Kept { label: "blue", by: 3 })
  # The retained label is visible to the view, while the model field is exactly
  # the mutable counter codec's answer and contains no retained envelope.
  assert str.starts_with(out, "{\"ok\":true,\"model\":\"0\",\"patches\":[")
  assert str.contains(out, "[\"data-state\",\"blue\"]")

  let installed = Kept { label: "blue", by: 3 }
  let event = "{\"op\":\"event\",\"model\":\"1\",\"path\":[],\"event\":\"click\"}"
  let (event_replacement, event_out) = ask_state(event, Some(installed))
  # Event can read the state (the step is +3), but it has no API with which to
  # replace it. None means the session pump leaves its existing root untouched.
  assert event_replacement == None
  assert str.contains(event_out, "\"model\":\"4\"")
  assert str.contains(event_out, "\"s\":\"4\"")
}

test "a retained event needs init, and a later init replaces the state" {
  let event = "{\"op\":\"event\",\"model\":\"1\",\"path\":[],\"event\":\"click\"}"
  let (missing, refused) = ask_state(event, None)
  assert missing == None
  assert refused ==
    "{\"ok\":false,\"kind\":\"bad-request\"," ++
    "\"error\":\"an `event` needs a successful `init` first\"}"

  let old = Kept { label: "old", by: 99 }
  let (replacement, out) = ask_state("{\"op\":\"init\"}", Some(old))
  assert replacement == Some(Kept { label: "plain", by: 1 })
  assert str.contains(out, "[\"data-state\",\"plain\"]")

  # A decoder refusal is not an init and therefore proposes no replacement.
  let (bad, _error) = ask_state("{\"op\":\"init\",\"flags\":7}", Some(old))
  assert bad == None
}

test "an init with no flags is the init every host sends today" {
  # The compatibility assertion at the turn rather than at the decoder: an
  # application that takes flags must still answer the bare line, and what it
  # gets is `None` rather than an empty string it would have to tell apart
  # from a host that sent one.
  assert ask_flagged("{\"op\":\"init\"}") == ask("{\"op\":\"init\"}")
  assert str.contains(ask_flagged("{\"op\":\"init\"}"), "\"model\":\"0\"")
}

test "`turn` ignores flags, so an application that never asked for them is unmoved" {
  # `turn` is `turn_with_flags` with a constant `init`, and this is the
  # assertion that says the constant really is one: the same reply for a line
  # carrying flags and a line carrying none, byte for byte.
  assert ask("{\"op\":\"init\",\"flags\":\"7\"}") == ask("{\"op\":\"init\"}")
}

test "flags that are not a string are refused, and no model comes back" {
  let out = ask_flagged("{\"op\":\"init\",\"flags\":7}")
  assert str.starts_with(out, "{\"ok\":false,\"kind\":\"bad-request\"")
  assert not str.contains(out, "\"model\"")
  assert not str.contains(out, "\"patches\"")
}

test "an event turn is the same turn whether or not init takes flags" {
  # Flags are an init-time thing and nothing else: every later turn decodes
  # the model off the wire, so the two entry points cannot diverge after the
  # first line and this says so.
  let line = "{\"op\":\"event\",\"model\":\"41\",\"path\":[2],\"event\":\"click\"}"
  assert ask_flagged(line) == ask(line)
  let missing = "{\"op\":\"event\",\"model\":\"7\",\"path\":[3],\"event\":\"input\"}"
  assert ask_flagged(missing) == ask(missing)
}

test "an event folds one message in and answers the minimal patch" {
  # Clicking `+` changes one text node three levels down. The two buttons and
  # the containers are unchanged and no patch may mention them.
  let out = ask("{\"op\":\"event\",\"model\":\"41\",\"path\":[2],\"event\":\"click\"}")
  assert out ==
    "{\"ok\":true,\"model\":\"42\",\"patches\":" ++
    "[{\"path\":[1,0],\"op\":\"replace\",\"node\":{\"t\":\"text\",\"s\":\"42\"}}]}"
}

test "the other button means the other message, at the same tree" {
  let out = ask("{\"op\":\"event\",\"model\":\"41\",\"path\":[0],\"event\":\"click\"}")
  assert str.contains(out, "\"model\":\"40\"")
  assert str.contains(out, "\"s\":\"40\"")
}

test "an address that listens for nothing is an error, not a silent no-op" {
  let out = ask("{\"op\":\"event\",\"model\":\"1\",\"path\":[1],\"event\":\"click\"}")
  assert out ==
    "{\"ok\":false,\"kind\":\"no-handler\"," ++
    "\"error\":\"nothing at [1] listens for `click`\"}"
  let root = ask("{\"op\":\"event\",\"model\":\"1\",\"path\":[],\"event\":\"click\"}")
  assert str.contains(root, "nothing at [] listens for `click`")
}

test "a malformed line is answered, and the model is not mentioned" {
  let out = ask("{\"op\":\"event\"}")
  assert str.starts_with(out, "{\"ok\":false,\"kind\":\"bad-request\"")
  assert not str.contains(out, "\"model\"")
  assert not str.contains(out, "\"patches\"")
}

test "a turn is a function of its line, so a transcript replays" {
  let line = "{\"op\":\"event\",\"model\":\"7\",\"path\":[2],\"event\":\"click\"}"
  assert ask(line) == ask(line)
  # With a payload too: the string is a field of the line like any other, so
  # replaying the line replays the message it produced.
  let typed = "{\"op\":\"event\",\"model\":\"7\",\"path\":[3],\"event\":\"input\",\"payload\":\"12\"}"
  assert ask(typed) == ask(typed)
}

test "a payload reaches update through the listener's own function" {
  # The whole path, end to end: the host's text goes to the function the
  # listener at [3] named, `update` reads what came back, and the model changed
  # to something no click could have produced. Nothing named a message on the
  # way, and nothing outside the view decided which constructor it lands in.
  let out = ask("{\"op\":\"event\",\"model\":\"7\",\"path\":[3],\"event\":\"input\",\"payload\":\"12\"}")
  assert str.contains(out, "\"model\":\"12\"")
  assert str.contains(out, "\"s\":\"12\"")
  # The text is carried verbatim, not parsed by the boundary: a payload that
  # is not a number is a message `update` decides to ignore, not an error.
  let junk =
    ask("{\"op\":\"event\",\"model\":\"7\",\"path\":[3],\"event\":\"input\",\"payload\":\"twelve\"}")
  assert str.contains(junk, "\"model\":\"7\"")
  assert str.contains(junk, "\"patches\":[]")
}

test "what an event means at an address is a question with an answer" {
  # The dispatch-level assertion, which is what replaces "compare the trees and
  # read the message out of the listener". `route.at` says which listener the
  # host's pair resolves to, `node.deliver` says what it makes of a payload,
  # and together they are what `settle` does minus the refusals.
  let tree = vw(Counter { n: 7 })
  match route.at(tree, [3], "input") {
    None -> { assert false }
    Some(l) -> {
      assert deliver(l, "12") == Set(text: "12")
      # Verbatim: the boundary hands over the string it was given and the
      # application decides what it is worth.
      assert deliver(l, "twelve") == Set(text: "twelve")
    }
  }
  match route.at(tree, [2], "click") {
    None -> { assert false }
    Some(l) -> {
      # A `NoData` listener ignores what it is handed, which is why `settle`
      # can pass `""` rather than carry a second shape for the argument.
      assert deliver(l, "") == Inc
      assert deliver(l, "ignored") == Inc
    }
  }
}

test "a payload that does not match what the listener asked for is refused" {
  # The listener at [3] asks for a value; a line with none is a host holding a
  # tree this model does not produce, and the answer is the one `no-handler`
  # gets rather than a message built from a default.
  let missing = ask("{\"op\":\"event\",\"model\":\"7\",\"path\":[3],\"event\":\"input\"}")
  assert missing ==
    "{\"ok\":false,\"kind\":\"bad-request\"," ++
    "\"error\":\"`input` asks for a payload, but none arrived\"}"
  # And the other way round: the buttons ask for nothing, so a host that sends
  # something is a host reading the wrong element.
  let extra =
    ask("{\"op\":\"event\",\"model\":\"7\",\"path\":[2],\"event\":\"click\",\"payload\":\"x\"}")
  assert extra ==
    "{\"ok\":false,\"kind\":\"bad-request\"," ++
    "\"error\":\"`click` asks for no payload, but one arrived\"}"
  # A refused turn carries no model and no patches, so the page is untouched.
  assert not str.contains(extra, "\"patches\"")
}
